.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt ELFCACHE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm elfcache
.Nd construct and inspect signed system ELF library caches
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm build
.Fl r Ar staged-root
.Fl o Ar cache
.Op Fl k Ar private.pem
.Op Fl l Ar library-list
.Nm
.Cm inspect
.Ar cache
.Nm
.Cm map
.Op Fl j
.Ar cache
.Nm
.Cm info
.Op Fl j
.Ar cache
.Nm
.Cm verify
.Op Fl j
.Op Fl p Ar public.pem
.Ar cache
.Nm
.Cm extract
.Op Fl p Ar public.pem
.Fl o Ar new-directory
.Ar cache
.Nm
.Cm layout
.Op Fl k Ar private.pem
.Op Fl p Ar public.pem
.Fl o Ar sidecar
.Ar cache
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The build command collects ELF shared-library files from the staged root's
.Pa /lib
and
.Pa /usr/lib
into a deterministic cache.
It preserves the ELF bytes preceding any per-image signature container,
aligns image slices to 64 KiB, and
stores identical input bytes once.
Library names are scoped to their original directory.
With
.Fl l ,
include only paths named in the newline-separated selection file.
Entries must be direct /lib or /usr/lib library paths; blank lines and lines
beginning with # are ignored.
Duplicate entries, unsafe paths, missing libraries and non-ELF selections
are errors.
Selection does not automatically add aliases or dependencies: use the static
dependency audit and runtime loader profiles to review the chosen set.
Libraries outside the cache retain ordinary loader behavior.
Library state, handles, relocations and writable data are not preinitialized.
.Pp
The staged root must be quiescent.
The builder detects source changes while copying and rejects paths escaping
the staged root.
Output paths must be new generation names, never active cache files.
With a signing key, it writes a detached
.Pa .sig
file using the library-cache protocol domain.
Publication of the cache and its signature into an active generation is a
separate operation.
.Pp
The inspect command validates structural bounds and prints entries, offsets
and sizes; its output is not a claim of authenticity.
The list and map commands are aliases for inspect: the map describes file
slices, not fixed process virtual addresses.
The info command reports entry and unique image counts, total named input
bytes, unique payload bytes, cache file size and format/alignment overhead.
These structural commands do not authenticate the cache.
They reject verification-key options instead of silently ignoring them.
Options that do not apply to a command are usage errors.
Alias deduplication is not a memory-saving measurement; ordinary shared
libraries already share file-backed pages.
The verify command additionally authenticates the cache signature and checks
every entry's SHA-256 digest in a private snapshot.
With
.Fl j ,
info and verify emit one JSON object using schema
.Dq org.5bsd.elfcache.summary.v1 .
The object includes counts, byte totals, format version, authentication and
payload-digest verification booleans, and verification-key source.
Info reports both verification booleans as false; verify reports true only
after successful authentication and validation of every entry.
On failure, neither command emits a JSON object.
Map with
.Fl j
emits schema
.Dq org.5bsd.elfcache.images.v1
with an image array containing paths, payload SHA-256 digests, cache offsets,
payload and original source lengths, and zero-based canonical image indexes.
Aliases refer to the first entry sharing the same payload slice.
Paths use byte-values encoding: non-ASCII filesystem bytes are escaped as
U+00xx; consumers recover the original bytes from those values rather than
encoding them as UTF-8.
The hashes describe the index and are not verified by structural inspection;
both verification booleans are false.
No image JSON is emitted until all index records pass structural checks.
Offsets locate bytes in the cache file, not process virtual addresses.
The JSON option is not accepted by other commands.
These summaries describe stored bytes, not resident memory or runtime
capability authority.
.Pp
The extract command authenticates a private snapshot of the cache and checks
all entry digests before creating a new destination directory.
It writes each unsigned ELF payload under
.Pa lib
or
.Pa usr/lib
and verifies the extracted bytes.
Aliases become separate byte-identical regular files with mode 0444;
original symlink relationships and filesystem metadata are not recorded.
Existing destinations, including symlinks, are refused.
The destination root has mode 0700.
On an I/O failure, a partial destination may remain and must be removed before
retrying; no existing files are overwritten.
Extraction does not install or activate the libraries.
Without an explicit public key, verification uses the system trust catalogue.
.Pp
Cache format version 1 excludes each source library's appended signature and
policy container.
The cache has its own detached signature.
Extraction produces unsigned ELF files, not the original signed artifacts.
The loader retains source-file identity and checks cached ELF bytes against
the source, using separate source and payload lengths.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/libdata/5bsd/cache/elf.cache.sig
.It Pa /usr/libdata/5bsd/cache/elf.cache
System cache generation selected at startup.
.It Pa /usr/libdata/5bsd/cache/elf.cache.sig
Detached system cache signature.
.El
.Sh OPTIONAL SHARED RELRO DOMAIN
The
.Cm layout
command creates a separate signed relative-layout sidecar bound to the full
cache generation by SHA-256.
It leaves cache version 1 unchanged.
With
.Fl p ,
it first verifies the input cache signature.
The sidecar records eligible ELF64 little-endian amd64 objects, their load
extents and nonexecutable GNU RELRO ranges.
Unsupported images retain ordinary loader behavior.
Its signature uses the distinct
.Dq org.5bsd.elf-layout.v1
protocol domain.
.Pp
SwitchBoard can explicitly enable this optimization in
.Pa /Capabilities/Config/switchboard/elf-cache-domain.ucl .
The root-owned configuration names the signed sidecar, exact system service
labels, and seed library basenames.
An example is installed with the SwitchBoard examples.
The default is disabled; absent configuration enrolls no services.
An enabled configuration must name at least one service and library.
.Pp
The legacy top-level
.Cm layout , signature , services ,
and
.Cm libraries
fields describe one domain.
Alternatively,
.Cm domains
is an array of up to eight objects, each containing those four fields.
Do not mix the two forms.
A service may belong to only one domain; at most 64 exact service labels may
be selected in total, with up to 64 seed libraries per domain.
Unknown or duplicate fields, duplicate memberships, invalid strings and
excessive configurations are rejected before any domain is created.
The root-owned configuration remains limited to 16 KiB.
.Pp
For example, two distinct authenticated domains can use the same signed
relative layout
and cache generation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
enabled = true;
domains = [
  { layout = "/usr/libdata/5bsd/cache/elf.layout";
    signature = "/usr/libdata/5bsd/cache/elf.layout.sig";
    services = ["system.Example/first"];
    libraries = ["libservice.so.1"]; },
  { layout = "/usr/libdata/5bsd/cache/elf.layout";
    signature = "/usr/libdata/5bsd/cache/elf.layout.sig";
    services = ["system.Example/second"];
    libraries = ["libservice.so.1"]; }
];
.Ed
.Pp
Each entry uses a separately created issuer-owned domain and RELRO backing.
Reusing the signed layout preserves relative library slots.
The kernel still chooses an independent randomized base for each domain;
this configuration does not force identical absolute library addresses.
It does not merge domain identity or alias independent backing.
The broker exposes the new membership table only after every domain has been
seeded and published successfully.
Failure closes staged handles and leaves any previously selected table intact.
Seeds still have an individual 30-second deadline, so eight domains may require
up to eight such waits.
Personal installations are never enrolled by these system service labels.
.Pp
The issuer pins and verifies the cache generation and sidecar, creates a
randomized domain base for the authenticated layout, and launches a signed
executable-constrained
.Pa /usr/libexec/elfcache-seed
helper.
The helper links only the base C runtime and loads the configured libraries;
it does not carry the builder's cryptographic dependencies.
The seed has capture rights only.
After successful seed exit, the issuer seals the backing against writes,
growth and truncation and validates it before publication.
The seed wait is bounded to 30 seconds.
Configured startup failures are reported as cache initialization failures;
there is no silently accepted unauthenticated generation.
.Pp
Enrolled loaders still verify source signatures, compare cache source bytes,
perform all ordinary ELF relocations, and resolve IFUNC and TLS state.
Only exactly equal, locally relocated RELRO pages receive private read-only
mappings of canonical backing.
Different symbol bindings, lazy/eager state, TLS offsets, unavailable entries
and address collisions preserve ordinary private state.
Main executable, stack and heap randomization remain independent.
No environment variable selects or grants domain membership.
.Pp
Libraries within a domain share addresses, reducing independence of address
randomization between its members.
Choose membership accordingly.
Revocation prevents fresh descriptor acquisition; existing mappings and
already delegated descriptors retain their lifetime.
The generation remains pinned until its domains, grants and mappings release
it.
Disk packing totals and matching-page counts are not resident-memory savings;
include canonical backing and page-table costs in measurements.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libcapimage 3 ,
.Xr capsign 8
.Sh LAYOUT VERIFICATION
The
.Cm layout-verify
.Fl p Ar public-key
.Fl l Ar layout
.Ar cache
command requires valid signatures on both the layout and cache, checks the
layout geometry against the cached ELF images, and verifies that the layout
names the exact cache generation by its full SHA-256 digest.
It authenticates private temporary copies so verification and parsing inspect
the same bytes even if the input paths are replaced concurrently.
Temporary disk space proportional to the cache size is required.
This command verifies artifacts; it does not enable a sharing domain or grant
process authority.
